From de9a51cf8828aa8223d1bce026ab4e6058785201 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 06:49:16 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] [clangd] The owned module cache and module workers follow
 what the system provides, measured in the cache directory

The owned module cache (ModuleBuildCache) and the default of supervised
module workers were compiled for Linux alone (`#ifdef __linux__`). Every
other system, and clangd built as an openkal ok image, compiled each
prerequisite in clangd and copied the published BMI for each reader, although
macOS and the C library of an ok image provide what the owned cache uses.

ModuleCachePlatform names what the owned cache needs and states which of it
this system provides: a byte-range lock held by an open file, so that another
open of the same file excludes it (F_OFD_SETLK; macOS has it under the same
command numbers without naming them); files and directories made, opened and
removed relative to an open directory; and a name removed while open. It also
states how a group of workers can be held to a memory budget (Linux: a
delegated cgroup v2), and supplies the operations whose form differs between
systems: the lock, syncing a file's data and a directory's names, the owner
and permission word, the count of names, and the modification time. The
owned cache is written against these and is compiled wherever POSIX names
exist; Windows compiles none of it and answers that it provides none.

Where the system provides it, the defaults are supervised workers and 1024
MiB of owned payloads; elsewhere they remain compilation in clangd and a copy
for each reader. Compiling in clangd uses no owned cache unless one is asked
for. A volume can lack what its system provides, so a module cache directory
is measured once before its owned cache is opened: a scratch file and
directory there are locked through two open files, synced and removed while
open. A directory that fails is cached by the portable path, and the log
states why ("Module cache <root>: a copy of each BMI for each reader; the
owned cache is not used here: ..."); one that passes logs "Module cache
<root>: owned". The configuration is logged once, with what the system
provides.

A reader of the owned cache holds an open file for each module it reads (its
lease), and a worker one for each prerequisite. The number of files a process
may hold open is raised to the most its system allows (on macOS, OPEN_MAX at
most) and logged with the configuration, and the leases of a worker share one
open cache of their root, so that a lease adds its own open file alone. A
cache for each lease opened the root's directory and ledger again for each
prerequisite, which exceeded the 256 open files macOS gives a process by
default, and the 64 open directories of an ok image, for a module that
imports 175. A generation marker that cannot be opened reports why.

Each module built in a worker is logged with the worker's time, processor
time and peak memory ("Module worker built <module> in N ms: CPU N ms, peak N
MiB").
